## Next.js 16 Canary App Router Migration Guide

Based on the documentation for Next.js 16 canary (`next@^16.2.0-canary.41`), here's a comprehensive guide for migrating from Pages Router to App Router:

---

## 1. File Structure for App Directory

The App Router uses a **file-system based routing** system where folders and files determine your routes.

### Directory Layout
```
app/
├── layout.tsx              # Root layout (required)
├── page.tsx                # Home page (/)
├── favicon.ico             # Favicon
├── opengraph-image.jpg     # OG image
├── blog/
│   ├── layout.tsx          # Blog layout (optional)
│   ├── page.tsx            # /blog
│   └── [slug]/
│       ├── page.tsx        # /blog/[slug] (dynamic)
│       └── opengraph-image.tsx # Dynamic OG image
└── (marketing)/            # Route group (doesn't appear in URL)
    └── layout.tsx          # Separate layout
```

### Key Conventions
- **Folders** = URL segments
- **Routes require either `page.tsx` or `route.ts`** to be publicly accessible
- **Private folders** start with underscore: `_components`, `_lib`
- **Route groups** use parentheses: `(marketing)` - doesn't affect URL

---

## 2. Root Layout (app/layout.tsx)

The root layout is **required** and must contain `<html>` and `<body>` tags.

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export default function RootLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>
        {/* Navigation, providers, etc. */}
        {children}
      </body>
    </html>
  )
}
```

**Critical Notes:**
- Root layout **must** define `<html>` and `<body>` tags
- Do **NOT** manually add `<head>` tags - use the Metadata API instead
- Layouts are **cached during navigation** and don't re-render
- You **cannot** access the raw request object; use `headers()` and `cookies()` APIs instead
- Layouts cannot receive `searchParams` - use pages or Client Components for that

---

## 3. Page Components (app/page.tsx)

Page components define unique UI for specific routes. They're always **Server Components by default**.

```tsx
// app/page.tsx
export default function Page() {
  return <h1>Hello, Next.js!</h1>
}
```

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx - Dynamic routes
export default async function Page({
  params,
  searchParams,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
  searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const filters = (await searchParams).filters
  return <h1>Blog Post: {slug}</h1>
}
```

**Important Changes from Pages Router:**
- `params` and `searchParams` are now **promises** (you must `await` them)
- Pages are **Server Components by default** (no need for `getServerSideProps`)
- **TypeScript:** Use `PageProps<'/route/[param]'>` helper for type safety (generated during `next dev`)

---

## 4. Metadata (Replacing next/head)

The App Router uses the **Metadata API** instead of `next/head`. Metadata is now code-first.

### Static Metadata
```tsx
// app/blog/layout.tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'My Blog',
  description: 'Welcome to my blog',
  openGraph: {
    title: 'My Blog',
    description: 'Welcome to my blog',
    images: ['/og-image.jpg'],
  },
}

export default function BlogLayout({
  children,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode
}) {
  return <section>{children}</section>
}
```

### Dynamic Metadata
```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
import type { Metadata, ResolvingMetadata } from 'next'

type Props = {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
  searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>
}

export async function generateMetadata(
  { params, searchParams }: Props,
  parent: ResolvingMetadata
): Promise<Metadata> {
  const slug = (await params).slug
  const post = await fetch(`/api/blog/${slug}`).then(res => res.json())

  return {
    title: post.title,
    description: post.description,
  }
}

export default function Page({ params, searchParams }: Props) {
  // Your page component
}
```

### File-Based Metadata
- `favicon.ico` - Favicon
- `icon.jpg`, `apple-icon.jpg` - App icons
- `opengraph-image.jpg`, `twitter-image.jpg` - Social media images
- `robots.txt` - SEO robots file
- `sitemap.xml` - SEO sitemap

**Note:** Metadata automatically handles `<head>` tag generation - never manually add `<title>` or `<meta>` tags to layouts.

---

## 5. Server Components vs Client Components

**This is the biggest paradigm shift.**

### Server Components (Default)
- Can access databases, secrets, and backend resources directly
- Cannot use state, event handlers, or browser APIs
- Data is fetched on the server
- Reduces JavaScript sent to browser

```tsx
// app/blog/page.tsx - Server Component by default
export default async function BlogPage() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://api.example.com/posts').then(r => r.json())
  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map(post => <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>)}
    </ul>
  )
}
```

### Client Components
- Use `'use client'` directive at top of file
- Can use state, event handlers, browser APIs, hooks
- Cannot directly access backend resources/secrets
- Imported by Server Components and passed as children

```tsx
// app/ui/counter.tsx
'use client'

import { useState } from 'react'

export default function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0)
  return (
    <div>
      <p>{count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

### Composition Pattern
```tsx
// app/page.tsx - Server Component
import Counter from '@/app/ui/counter' // Client Component

export default async function Page() {
  const data = await fetchData() // Only works in Server Components

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{data.title}</h1>
      <Counter /> {/* Client Component works here */}
    </div>
  )
}
```

**Key Rule:** Server Components can contain Client Components, but Client Components cannot directly contain async Server Components (though they can receive them as children).

---

## 6. Dynamic Routes

Dynamic segments use square brackets.

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
// Matches: /blog/first-post, /blog/another-post, etc.

// app/shop/[category]/[item]/page.tsx
// Matches: /shop/electronics/phone

// app/docs/[...slug]/page.tsx
// Catch-all: /docs/intro, /docs/setup/installation

// app/api/[[...route]]/route.ts
// Optional catch-all: /api, /api/users, /api/users/123
```

---

## 7. Breaking Changes & Deprecation Notices (Next.js 16 Canary)

### Critical Changes from Next.js 15 → 16

**1. Promises for Dynamic Parameters**
- `params` and `searchParams` are now **promises**
- Must `await` them in async components
- Client Components must use `use(params)` from React

```tsx
// Old (Next.js 14)
export default function Page({ params }) {
  const slug = params.slug // Direct access
}

// New (Next.js 16)
export default async function Page({ params }: { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> }) {
  const { slug } = await params // Must await
}
```

**Codemod available:**
```bash
npx @next/codemod@canary upgrade 15-to-16
```

**2. Linting Changes**
- `next build` **no longer runs linter automatically**
- Run ESLint directly via npm scripts
- ESLint now supports newer `eslint.config.mjs` format (recommended)

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "lint": "eslint",
    "lint:fix": "eslint --fix"
  }
}
```

**3. Turbopack is Default Bundler**
- Turbopack is now the default bundler (much faster)
- To use Webpack: `next dev --webpack` or `next build --webpack`

**4. React Canary Requirement**
- App Router requires React canary releases (includes React 19 features)
- Includes Server Components, Suspense, and Server Actions built-in

**5. New Canary Features** (available now in `next@canary`)
- `forbidden()` and `unauthorized()` functions for auth
- `forbidden.js` and `unauthorized.js` error pages
- New auth configuration options

---

## 8. Component Rendering Hierarchy

When you have nested routes, components render in this order:
1. `layout.js`
2. `template.js` (re-rendered on each navigation)
3. `error.js` (React error boundary)
4. `loading.js` (React Suspense boundary)
5. `not-found.js`
6. `page.js` or child `layout.js`

---

## 9. Nested Layouts

Layouts at each level wrap their child routes.

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx - Wraps everything
export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html>
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  )
}

// app/blog/layout.tsx - Wraps /blog and descendants
export default function BlogLayout({ children }) {
  return <section>{children}</section>
}

// app/blog/page.tsx - /blog
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx - /blog/[slug]
```

---

## 10. Migration Checklist

- [ ] Create `app/` directory
- [ ] Create root `app/layout.tsx` with `<html>` and `<body>`
- [ ] Create `app/page.tsx` (replaces `pages/index.js`)
- [ ] Convert page files: `pages/blog/[slug].js` → `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx`
- [ ] Update imports: use relative or alias imports (`@/`)
- [ ] Convert `next/head` to Metadata API
- [ ] Convert `getServerSideProps`/`getStaticProps` to Server Components (fetch in component)
- [ ] Mark interactive components with `'use client'`
- [ ] Update `getStaticPaths` to use `generateStaticParams()`
- [ ] Update environment variables to use `process.env` (no more `NEXT_PUBLIC_` required)
- [ ] Test with `npm run dev`
- [ ] Update ESLint to new format
- [ ] Remove `next lint` from build scripts

---

## Key Takeaways

1. **Server Components First** - Components are Server Components by default; only add `'use client'` when needed
2. **Metadata API** - Use exported `metadata` object or `generateMetadata()` function instead of `next/head`
3. **Promises** - `params` and `searchParams` are promises that must be awaited
4. **File-system Routing** - Routes determined by folder structure in `app/` directory
5. **Root Layout Required** - Must have `app/layout.tsx` with `<html>` and `<body>`
6. **Breaking Changes** - Read docs before writing code; APIs differ from training data

---

This should give you everything you need for a successful migration. The documentation emphasizes that **this is NOT the Next.js you know** if you've used older versions - make sure to read the relevant docs while coding!

